<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>End to End Throughput</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ A.4 End to End Throughput ‣ Appendix A Appendix" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> shows the end-to-end throughput gain for Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501 and Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct when executed on FlexiCache compared to the vLLM baseline. The workload consists of 100 random inputs from L-Eval, each with input lengths ranging from 10k to 30k tokens and an output length of 500. FlexiCache achieves a 1.37× improvement, indicating that performance gains extend to larger models as well.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2511.00868v1/token_throughput_mistral_qwen.svg" id="A1.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:538/384;" width="538" height="384"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span><span id="A1.F10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decode Speed vs. Batch Size.</span> Each input has 10k tokens. FlexiCache achieves greater speedups as batch size increases.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
